course: dictionary
lesson: 9087
title: Thick, wet and dirty: sludge
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Sludge is thick, soft, wet mud, or a mix of mud and dirty liquid. You can find clues to its meaning in the words around it. Look at where it is and what it does. «...the sludge can be turned into gas for energy.» Something that can be turned into gas must be a kind of waste, not clean water.
explain: Sludge collects at the bottom of ponds, gutters and drains. If you have ever seen a gutter after heavy rain, you may have seen the dark, gooey stuff that builds up there. That is sludge.
explain: Always check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «...the thick, dirty stuff can be turned into gas for energy.» It still makes sense, so you have found the meaning.
fact: Sludge means thick, soft, wet mud or a mix of mud and dirty liquid.
fact: Sludge can also mean the thick, dirty stuff left after cleaning water, such as sewage sludge.
fact: At a sewage works, «the sludge can be turned into gas for energy.»
fact: Informally, sludge can mean any thick, unpleasant mess, like soggy food or slushy snow.
fact: Useful phrases: green sludge, a layer of sludge, oil sludge.
exercise: In the sewage works, what is the sludge? | Dry sand from the beach | A kind of bright, sparkling gas | *Thick, dirty stuff left after cleaning water | Clean water ready to drink
exercise: Her welly sank into the pond bottom, which was thick, soft and wet. What is the word for that stuff? | sparkle | sandwich | *sludge | sunshine
exercise: Where is sludge most likely to collect? | A dry cupboard full of tins | A bright blue sky | A glass of clean water | *A gutter after heavy rain
test: Mum says her soup looked like green sludge. What does sludge mean here? | *A thick, unpleasant mess, like soggy food | Sunshine on a hill | A sweet, sticky sauce | A clean glass of water
test: What does sewage sludge mean? | A sweet, sticky sauce | *Thick, dirty stuff left after cleaning water | Fresh, clean drinking water | A pile of dry leaves
exam: Which sentence uses sludge correctly? | The sludge was bright and sunny on the hill. | She ate a sludge of clean, dry biscuits. | *After the storm, the road was covered in a layer of sludge. | The sludge flew high into the blue sky.
